.TH STASH 1 "September 2026" "stash" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
stash \- turn Linux system telemetry into sound
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stash
.I SOURCE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B stash \-l
.RI [ PREFIX ]
.br
.B stash \-i
.I SOURCE
.br
.B stash \-p
.I PRIMITIVE
.br
.B stash
.RB ( \-h | \-\-help )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stash
(Sound Telemetry Auto SHell) reads live Linux hardware and operating-system
telemetry.  With no sound-producing option it writes numeric samples to
standard output.  Sound options turn the source into a control signal for a
persistent oscillator, note events, rhythm, and an ordered effects chain.
Csound provides audio-device and raw-PCM synthesis.
.PP
.I SOURCE
is a canonical source name such as
.BR cpu.usage ,
.BR cpu.cores.usage ,
.BR ram.used ,
or
.BR net.enp4s0.rx .
Use
.B \-
as the source to read numeric samples from standard input.
.SH COMMAND FORMS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a concise command reference to standard output and exit successfully.
The help flag must be the only argument.
.TP
.BR \-l " [" PREFIX "]"
List known sources, optionally restricted by a case-sensitive name prefix.
Unavailable hardware-specific sources are identified as such.
.TP
.BI \-i " SOURCE"
Show a source's name, scalar or vector kind, unit, natural range, and local
availability.
.TP
.BI \-p " PRIMITIVE"
Resolve and inspect a note, note list, scale, mode, or tempo-bearing rhythm.
.SH OPTIONS
Options in this section follow
.IR SOURCE .
.TP
.BI \-w " WAVE"
Select
.BR sine ,
.BR square ,
.BR saw ,
.BR tri ,
or
.BR noise .
The default is
.BR sine .
.TP
.BI \-m " [CONTROL:]TARGET=MAP"
Add a modulation.  Omitted
.I CONTROL
means the primary source.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-range " [CONTROL=]MIN..MAX"
Override the normalization range of a control.  Omitted
.I CONTROL
means the primary source.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-v " GAIN"
Set static output gain in the inclusive range 0 through 1.  The default is
0.1.
.TP
.BI \-t " TRIGGER"
Define a threshold trigger:
.BR above:VALUE ,
.BR below:VALUE ,
.BR rise:VALUE ,
or
.BR fall:VALUE .
Vector-source triggers are evaluated independently at each index.
.TP
.BI \-n " NOTES"
Set note material.  Accepts one scientific-pitch note, a comma-separated note
list, a scale, or a mode.  Vector index N maps to note index N.
.TP
.BI \-r " RHYTHM"
Set a rhythm in
.B rhythm:BPM:DIVISION:PATTERN
or
.B rhythm:DIVISION:PATTERN
form.  A rhythm without embedded BPM requires
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-b " BPM"
Set a positive tempo, overriding an embedded rhythm tempo.  Requires
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI \-d " TIME"
Set the event gate duration.  Durations use
.B ms
or
.B s
suffixes.  The default is 100ms.
.TP
.BI \-a " ATTACK,DECAY,SUSTAIN,RELEASE"
Set the amplitude envelope.  Attack, decay, and release are durations;
sustain is from 0 through 1.  The default is 5ms,20ms,.8,50ms.
.TP
.BI \-\-swing " AMOUNT"
Set alternating-subdivision swing from 50 through 75 percent.  The default is
50 (straight timing).  Requires
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI \-f " FILTER"
Append
.B lp:CUTOFF[,Q]
or
.B hp:CUTOFF[,Q]
to the effects chain.  The default Q is 0.707.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-x " EFFECT"
Append
.B delay:TIME,FEEDBACK,MIX
or
.B drive:AMOUNT
to the effects chain.  Delay feedback is 0 through 0.95; delay mix and drive
amount are 0 through 1.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.B \-o \-
Write headerless raw PCM to standard output instead of using the default audio
device.  The format is 48,000 Hz, two-channel, stereo-interleaved,
little-endian float32.
.SH MAPPINGS
A map has the form:
.PP
.RS
.B MIN..MAX[/CURVE][~SMOOTH]
.RE
.PP
.I CURVE
is
.BR linear ,
.BR exp ,
or
.BR log ;
the default is linear.
.I SMOOTH
is a duration such as 150ms; the default is 0ms.
.PP
Signal targets are
.BR freq ,
.BR gain ,
.BR pan ,
and
.BR gate .
Effect targets are
.BR filter.cutoff ,
.BR filter.q ,
.BR delay.time ,
.BR delay.feedback ,
.BR delay.mix ,
and
.BR drive.amount .
When several matching effects exist, an unindexed target addresses the most
recently declared matching effect.
.PP
A control may be a telemetry source or, when a rhythm is active,
.BR rhythm.gate ,
.BR rhythm.hit ,
.BR rhythm.step ,
.BR rhythm.velocity ,
or
.BR rhythm.phase .
.SH VALUE GRAMMAR
Numbers are decimal and may use
.BR k ,
.BR M ,
or
.B G
for powers of 1000.  A range is
.BR MIN..MAX ,
with its minimum strictly less than its maximum.  Durations require an
.B ms
or
.B s
suffix.
.PP
Notes use
.BR LETTER[ACCIDENTAL]OCTAVE ,
for example C4, C#4, or Db4, with A4 tuned to 440 Hz.
Scale and mode primitives use:
.PP
.RS
.B scale:ROOT:NAME:LENGTH
.br
.B mode:ROOT:NAME:LENGTH
.RE
.PP
Scales are major, minor, chromatic, pentatonic-major, and pentatonic-minor.
Modes are ionian, dorian, phrygian, lydian, mixolydian, aeolian, and locrian.
.PP
Rhythm divisions are 1/1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, and 1/32.  A pattern contains
.B x
for a hit and
.B \-
for a rest.
.SH OPERATING MODES
In telemetry mode, scalar sources write one value per line and vector sources
write one comma-separated vector per line.  No labels are emitted.
.PP
Any sound-producing option selects audio mode.  The default device receives
audio and standard output remains empty.  With
.BR \-o " " \- ,
raw PCM is written to standard output.  Diagnostics always go to standard
error.
.SH EXAMPLES
List CPU sources and inspect aggregate CPU usage:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash \-l cpu
stash \-i cpu.usage
.fi
.RE
.PP
Map CPU usage exponentially to a smoothed sine frequency:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash cpu.usage \-w sine \-m freq=80..2k/exp~150ms
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use newline-delimited standard input as a control:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf '0\en.5\en1\en' | stash \- \-\-range 0..1 \-m freq=100..2k
.fi
.RE
.PP
Play vector-triggered notes from a mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash cpu.cores.usage \-t above:95 \-n mode:E3:phrygian:12 \-d 150ms
.fi
.RE
.SH DEFAULTS
Waveform sine; frequency 440 Hz; gain 0.1; pan 0; mapping curve linear;
smoothing 0ms; event gate 100ms; ADSR 5ms,20ms,.8,50ms; filter Q 0.707;
swing 50; sample rate 48,000 Hz; two output channels.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success, including help and clean SIGINT or SIGTERM shutdown.
One indicates invalid syntax, unavailable input, initialization or execution
failure, or an output error.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM stop live telemetry or audio commands cleanly.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation and examples are available at
.UR https://github.com/zalmo/stash
.UE .
